\begin_layout Title
Securing Open Sound Control (OSC) over UDP:
 A Lightweight HMAC-Based Authentication Scheme for Embedded Systems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Paul V.
 Miller
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This project presents a lightweight authentication layer for OSC over UDP using HMAC and sequence-based replay protection.
 The approach is designed for resource-constrained embedded controllers that operate in live performance or real-time musical applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
OSC
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OSC (Open Sound Control) is widely used in computer music and in interactive computer systems,
 for sending data efficiently.
 Very frequently the data is some musical information (such as pitch,
 control data or velocity) that is produced by some sort of human interface.
 Many such software controllers are available such as TouchOSC or Protokol...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is also possible to build one's own physical controllers out of hardware components such as sliders,
 buttons,
 potentiometers,
 or even sensors ranging from the very inexpensive and ubiquitous ultrasonic distance sensor (HCSR04) to more complex sensors such as accellerometers,
 gyroscopes or magnetometers (e.g.
 LSM9DS1 or BNO055).
\end_layout

